    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "@@PAGE@@#faq",
      "url": "@@PAGE@@#faq",
      "isPartOf": {
        "@id": "@@PAGE@@#webpage"
      },
      "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do agencies actually need client onboarding software?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most don't. The real trigger is running more than roughly 10 concurrent onboardings with more than one internal team involved, not firm size or ambition. Below that line, a well-built template inside the project management tool you already pay for will usually outperform a new subscription.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best client onboarding software?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There isn't a single best one; it depends on your CRM and your pricing sensitivity. Rocketlane is the strongest all-rounder if you want onboarding plus ongoing delivery management in one tool. Arrows is the best fit if HubSpot is already your system of record. GUIDEcx suits high-volume, highly templated onboarding where consistency matters more than price transparency. Dock is the most accessible for a smaller team that wants a free tier to start on.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the best client onboarding solutions for small agencies?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a small agency, Dock's free tier or a cloned template inside Notion or ClickUp (see \"Automating client onboarding with the tools you already have\" above) will cover most needs without a seat-based contract. By contrast, accounting firms often need secure, document-heavy onboarding from the start. Most small agencies fail the decision test in this guide and don't need a paid tool at all.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens after onboarding ends?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Onboarding software's job finishes once the client is live. What happens afterward, relationship health, renewal risk, expansion opportunities, sits in a different category: client intelligence or customer success software. See \"Onboarding software vs. client intelligence\" above for how the two fit together.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does client onboarding software cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends heavily on the pricing model. Some tools offer a free plan, while paid plans vary by seat count, volume, or tier. Per-seat tools like Rocketlane ($49 to $69/user/month) and GUIDEcx (estimated $100-143/licence/month) scale with your internal team size. Dock ($350/month for 5 users, plus $50/month per extra seat, up to $1,000/month for Premium) is a flat tier plus $50 per extra seat. Volume-based tools like Arrows ($500/month) scale with how many onboardings you run, not your headcount. Model your own numbers using the worked example in the cost table above before comparing sticker prices.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can we automate onboarding with the tools we already have?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for most agencies under the volume threshold in the decision test, especially when the onboarding flow is a structured process. A cloned template board with a client-visible view and a CRM trigger, built once inside Notion, ClickUp or Asana, covers the core job a paid tool would otherwise do.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do we need it if we already have a project management tool?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if your PM tool's client-facing experience is weak and your onboarding volume is high enough to justify a dedicated layer. Dedicated tools are stronger when client-facing task tracking and progress tracking really matter. Most PM tools can approximate a client portal with a shared view; a dedicated onboarding tool is worth the extra cost mainly for its templates, automated nudges and CRM triggers, not for capabilities a PM tool entirely lacks.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will the team actually use it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "This is the real adoption risk, and it's worth testing in a 14-day pilot before signing an annual contract. The failure pattern is consistent across the tools we've seen agencies try: a tool that requires manual status updates gets treated exactly like the spreadsheet it replaced, and usage quietly drops within a quarter. Adoption is better when it removes repetitive work and shortens time to value. Ask every vendor, in the pilot, how much of the status update is automatic versus typed by a human.",
            "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowTo",
      "@id": "@@PAGE@@#howto",
      "name": "How to choose client onboarding software in seven steps",
      "inLanguage": "@@LANG@@",
      "step": [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "position": 1,
          "name": "Define the problem before the tool",
          "text": "Write down the actual symptom: late deliverables, lost context, client chasing you, admin eating account director time. A tool bought to fix a vague feeling never gets adopted."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "position": 2,
          "name": "Map your current stack",
          "text": "List what you already pay for: CRM, PM tool, e-signature, file sharing, and any customer onboarding tools you already use. Most of the features in this category already exist somewhere in your stack; the question is whether they're joined up."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "position": 3,
          "name": "Count clients, not seats, with a worked example",
          "text": "A 40-person agency onboarding 20 clients a year with 6 people touching each onboarding pays Rocketlane roughly $49 x 6 = $294/month on Standard. The same agency on Dock's Standard tier pays $400/month: the flat $350 covers 5 users, plus one extra seat at $50. Dock's pricing is a flat tier plus $50 per extra seat, not client volume. Run your own numbers before you shortlist."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "position": 4,
          "name": "Weigh manual entry against automatic capture, and the adoption risk that comes with each",
          "text": "A tool that requires account directors to manually update every status will be abandoned within a quarter, the same way the spreadsheet was. Ask every vendor to show you both how the tool tracks progress automatically and how it reduces repetitive tasks, versus what a human still has to type."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "position": 5,
          "name": "Run a 14-day pilot on five real accounts",
          "text": "Not a demo with sample data. Five live onboardings, with the account directors who'll actually use it, before you sign an annual contract."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "position": 6,
          "name": "Show the shortlist to everyone who has to live with it",
          "text": "The Head of Client Service needs to see it won't just replace the spreadsheet with more admin. The Head of Ops needs to see the team will actually open it. The person who signs the invoice needs the three-number pitch: cost, time saved, risk reduced."
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          "position": 7,
          "name": "Score the shortlist against your actual answers to steps 1 to 4",
          "text": ", and against time to value, not just a generic feature checklist. The tool with the most features is rarely the one that gets used."
        }
      ]
    },
